WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:06.350
In biology, transcription is the
process of copying DNA into RNA by an

2
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.470
enzyme called RNA polymerase.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:15.550
Generally, RNA polymerase only wants to
transcribe regions of DNA called genes,

4
00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.510
as these provide the correct
instructions for making useful RNA.

5
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:26.540
However, the human genome is 3.2
billion base pairs long, and only a

6
00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:31.590
small percentage of it contains genes.
So how does RNA polymerase know where

7
00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:32.560
to transcribe?

8
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.460
It makes use of what are called
promoters.

9
00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.090
Promoters are DNA sequences at the
beginning of genes that provide a

10
00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:47.400
landing pad for RNA polymerase and a
bunch of other proteins that

11
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.560
all together work to initiate the
transcription process.

12
00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:56.170
In other words, a promoter both marks
the start of the gene and helps bring

13
00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:57.700
it in for activation.

14
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.620
Although promoters for different genes
aren't exactly the same, they do share

15
00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:08.670
some common features. For instance, a
good fraction of promoters contain what

16
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.410
is called the TATA box, a sequence
characterized by alternating adenines

17
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:13.870
and thymines.

18
00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:18.650
This region of the promoter is
recognized by a protein that plays a

19
00:01:18.650 --> 00:01:23.050
role in stabilizing RNA polymerase and
encouraging its activity.

20
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.970
In addition to the promoter, some genes
make use of enhancer sequences. These

21
00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:34.630
are short sequences that recruit
so-called activator proteins that help

22
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.160
RNA polymerase work much more
efficiently.

23
00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.610
Fascinatingly, enhancers are often
located thousands of base pairs away,

24
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.940
and so the DNA must loop around to
allow for enhancers to get close to the

25
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:47.560
promoter.

26
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.550
Overall, by using enhancers and
promoters as genetic signals, the

27
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:58.030
transcription machinery within any
given cell is able to appropriately

28
00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.400
express its genes.

